'Done.\n\n**Change**\n- `src/components/TaskModal.tsx`: added `priority` local 
state initialized from `task.priority`; replaced the read-only `<p 
className="read-only-priority">` line with a `<label>Priority<select>` offering 
`Low` / `Medium` / `High`; `Save changes` now passes `{ ...task, title: 
title.trim() || task.title, status, priority }`. Cancel, backdrop `onMouseDown`,
and Escape paths were untouched, so all draft fields (including priority) are 
discarded, and the existing initial focus (`titleRef`) plus focus restoration in
the effect cleanup are preserved.\n- `src/types.ts`: extracted `TaskPriority = 
\'Low\' | \'Medium\' | \'High\'` and used it for `Task.priority` (no behavioral 
change).\n- `src/App.tsx`: no change needed — `updateTask` already replaces the 
whole task, so the selected-task detail reflects the new priority.\n\n**Commands
run**\n- `verify_frontend` → `npm run build && npm run test:public` (exit 0): 
`tsc --noEmit` + `vite build` succeeded; all 6 public Playwright tests 
passed.\n\n**Risks**\n- The `.read-only-priority` CSS class (if styled in 
`src/index.css`) is now unused; left in place to avoid unrelated CSS churn.\n- 
New priority behavior isn\'t covered by the existing public tests (they don\'t 
assert priority), so it\'s verified by build/types only.', 'aid': 0})
